  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Some stronger showers and storms possible this afternoon. \n\n-  Widespread showers increasing Sunday into Monday with \n   seasonably cool temperatures persisting into early next week.\n\n-  Gradual drying trend develops mid-week alongside morning \n   valley fog.\n\nShower activity will wane this morning giving way to partial \nafternoon clearing. Solar heating will generate weak to modest \ninstability, supporting spotty afternoon showers and thunderstorms\ncapable of brief heavy rainfall, lightning, hail, and erratic \nconvective outflow gusts of 25 to 45 mph. Persistent moderate\nshowers will continue tonight across western Montana. \n\nAn upper-level closed low will track directly across the Northern\nRockies Sunday through Monday before departing Saskatchewan-bound \nby Tuesday. Dynamic forcing and rich moisture advection will \nproduce widespread, soaking rainfall. Probabilities for rainfall \nexceeding 0.50 inches are very high between Sunday morning and \nTuesday morning reaching 80 to 90+ percent across West- Central \nand Northwest Montana, 60 to 80+ percent across North-Central \nIdaho, and as low as 60 percent for Southwest Montana. A 50 \npercent chance exists for total rainfall exceeding 2.00 inches \nfrom the Flathead Lake region extending north and east across the \nGlacier Region. Cold air entrainment aloft will lower snow levels,\nproducing some mountain snow accumulations above 9,000 feet.\n\nThe forecast becomes a bit uncertain toward the middle part of\nnext week, but what we know so far is:\nThe closed low exits to the northeast on Tuesday, allowing high\npressure to build over the region into the middle of next week.\nThis pattern will bring a drying trend with moderating\ntemperatures, though lingering boundary-layer moisture will\ncontinue to support overnight and early morning valley fog and \nlow stratus.",
